\title{AutoGraphForge: Conjecturing, Refutation and\\
Lean Formalization in One Loop}

\titlerunning{AutoGraphForge}

\author{J\'an Pastorek\orcidID{0000-0001-8237-1275}}
\authorrunning{J. Pastorek}

\institute{Department of Applied Informatics, Comenius University in Bratislava,
Slovakia\\
\email{jan.pastorek@fmph.uniba.sk}}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\textsf{AutoGraphForge} couples conjecturing, refuting, formalizing and proving
into one loop for graph theory. A counterexample-guided loop on an HPC cluster
produced $6{,}522$ conjectures surviving an implication-deciding novelty filter
over $559$ known relations and refutation against $348{,}207$ graphs; survivors
are exported to Lean~4 and handed to kernel-checked neural provers.
\keywords{Automated conjecturing \and Autoformalization \and Lean~4 \and
Counterexample search \and Mathematical knowledge management.}
\end{abstract}

\paragraph{Motivation.}
Conjecturing systems from \textsc{Graffiti}~\cite{fajtlowicz1988conjectures} to
\textsc{Graffiti}$^3$~\cite{davila2026graffiti3} and proof search over
Lean~4~\cite{moura2015lean} have developed separately; to our knowledge no
system joins conjecturing, refutation, formalization and proving into one loop.
\textsf{AutoGraphForge} is our attempt at one (code, data and logs at
\url{https://github.com/JanPastorek/AutoGraphForge}), answering the standing
failure modes---candidates false in general, known, or trivial---with adversarial
refutation, a machine-checkable knowledge base, and a kernel check.

\paragraph{Generation.}
\textsc{Graffiti}$^3$ emits bounds between graph invariants, and sufficient
conditions (graph-class predicates whose conjunction implies a target property),
over a \emph{small} evolving snapshot $T$ of graphs carrying $59$ exactly
computed \textsc{GraphCalc}~\cite{davila2025graphcalc} invariants: $45$ numeric
targets, $14$ boolean class predicates. Crucially $T$ grows \emph{only} by
counterexamples to its own conjectures, so generation stays cheap
while refutation ranges over a corpus three orders of magnitude larger.

\paragraph{Novelty as an LP feasibility test.} 
We curate $559$ classical, folklore and trivial relations, closed under
transitive composition and linear identity substitution, each tagged with the
graph class on which it holds. A candidate upper bound is \emph{redundant} when
it decomposes into a convex combination of tabled bounds on the same invariant,
non-negative multiples of known invariant lower bounds, and arbitrary multiples
of identities vanishing on the candidate's graph class (e.g.\ Gallai's
$n=\alpha+\tau$ on order, independence and vertex-cover numbers). Matching
coefficients symbolically makes the search for such a certificate a
linear-programming feasibility test, each tabled relation admitted only over the
candidate's whole hypothesis class. This makes ``is this already known?'' a
decidable query against a curated library.

\paragraph{Refutation, formalization, proving.}
Survivors face $348{,}207$ graphs unioning the House of Graphs
export~\cite{coolsaet2023house}, all connected graphs on at most nine vertices,
and extremal families, then random models, then six search backends that
maximise a candidate's violation margin~\cite{jooken2025computer}; every
counterexample enters $T$. Since a conjecture is already a structured
object---named invariants under class predicates---the Lean~4 export is
deterministic, yielding a \texttt{sorry}-terminated skeleton that elaborates to
a type-correct goal. \textsc{DeepSeek-Prover-V2-671B}~\cite{deepseek2025prover}
and \textsc{OProver-32B}~\cite{oprover2026} then search for proofs,
kernel-checked against pinned \texttt{mathlib4} and our invariant preamble, so
soundness is model-independent.

\paragraph{Results and status.}
Five $256$-core partitions split the $45$ targets and ran to a fixed point
($1.22$ CPU-years); notably the static datasets refuted far more candidates than
the search backends ($1$--$22$ counterexamples per round, at untuned defaults).
Deduplication left $6{,}522$ survivors, of which $33$ are flagged as
rediscoveries (K\H{o}nig--Egerv\'ary, Gallai)---a soundness check, not a
discovery. Auditing the $100$ survivors sharp on the most graphs gave $49$
recognised rediscoveries and $48$ not known to us to be trivial or false. Among
the latter is a pair relating the annihilation number $a$ (the largest $k$ whose
$k$ smallest degrees sum to at most the edge count) and the edge-cover number
$\mathrm{ec}$, which we then proved by hand: bipartite
$\Rightarrow\mathrm{ec}\le a$ and regular $\Rightarrow a\le\mathrm{ec}$, via
Gallai, K\H{o}nig and Pepper's $\alpha\le a$~\cite{pepper2004thesis}. The loop is
\emph{not yet closed}: the proving stage kernel-verifies trivial bounds but
awaits a controlled evaluation, and we expect it to be the hard part, since
invariants such as zero forcing exist only in our preamble and lie outside the
provers' training distribution---how to ground provers on freshly formalized
vocabulary is what we most hope to discuss.

